WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.120
Kubernetes adds orchestration boundaries and identities around containers.

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:07.620
Pods group one or more containers with shared context.

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:09.280
Nodes run pods.

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:12.780
Service accounts identify workloads to the API.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:18.920
RBAC determines allowed API actions, while admission controls evaluate objects before they are accepted.

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:24.980
Secrets, configuration, storage, and network policy connect workloads to other assets.

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:35.280
The threat model should identify which secrets a pod can receive, which services it can reach, which volumes it can mount, and which API operations its service account can perform.

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:40.380
Container escape risk connects to cluster risk through node and orchestration access.

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:45.960
A boundary failure on one node does not automatically mean total cluster compromise.

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:53.940
Blast radius depends on node credentials, service-account permissions, network paths, workload placement, and control-plane protections.

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:56.400
RBAC should minimize actions and scope.

00:00:56.620 --> 00:01:03.500
Admission policies can reject privileged settings, unsafe mounts, disallowed images, or missing security requirements.

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:10.840
Pod Security Standards provide a common baseline, but application-specific controls and justified exceptions are still necessary.

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:16.260
Network policies and segmentation limit workload communication, though they do not replace host isolation.

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:22.320
Namespace separation organizes resources but should not be treated as a complete hard-security boundary by itself.

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:25.020
Multi-tenant clusters require special care.

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:39.000
Separate workloads with different trust levels, consider dedicated nodes or clusters for high-risk boundaries, protect shared services, and test whether one tenant can affect another through identity, storage, network, or node dependencies.

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:42.100
The architecture should make expected containment explicit.
